        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.151,
            0.861,
            0.17
          ],
          "content": "y **Compare** your observations with the observations given in Table 3.3."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.177,
            0.862,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": "**Table 3.3: Your experiences, observations and questions from daily life**"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.227,
            0.201,
            0.885,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "| Experiences | Observations | Questions |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| When you get a smallcut on your skin | Red blood oozes out from the cut.A clot is formed after some time. | What causesblood to clot? |\n| When you get a skininfection | The area turns red and perhapsslightly swollen. You may have a fever. |  |\n| When you exerciseor run | You breathe faster. Your face mayturn red. |  |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_table_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.393,
            0.888,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": "The everyday experiences mentioned above are related to blood and its components."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.43,
            0.889,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": "1. The red colour of blood is due to haemoglobin, an iron-rich protein in the Red Blood Cells (RBCs). RBCs live for about 4 months and are replaced regularly."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.484,
            0.758,
            0.504
          ],
          "content": "2. Platelets help in blood clotting at the site of the injury."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.506,
            0.89,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": "3. During exercise or running, muscles need more oxygen, so breathing becomes faster and blood flow increases (face appears red)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.542,
            0.89,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "4. White Blood Cells (WBCs) collect at infected areas, causing pus formation and inflammation (causing redness and swelling, and possible pus formation at the infected area)."
        },
        {
